// Make a mesh consisting of a rectangle with circular particles
// The full rectangle, including the particles, is meshed.
// The space between the particles and the rectangle is physical volume 1
// The space inside the particles is physical volume 2
//
// The input parameters are:
//
//   ox, oy = the coordinates of the vertex with lower coordinates
//   lx, ly = the lengths of the box along the x-, y-directions
//   nobj = the number of particles
//   xp(i,:), yp(i,:) = the coordinates of the center of the circle i
//   rp(i) = the radius of the circle i
//   dx_box = the characteristic line element size on the box side
//   dx_part = the characteristic line element size on the particle surface
//
// NOTE: the parameters have to be defined before including this file.
//
// Points and curves are numbered as follows:
//
// P1 = (ox,    oy   )
// P2 = (ox+lx, oy   )
// P3 = (ox+lx, oy+ly)
// P4 = (ox,    oy+ly)
// C1 = P1-P2  ( parallel to x passing through oy )
// C2 = P2-P3  ( parallel to y passing through ox+lx )
// C3 = P3-P4  ( parallel to x passing through oy+ly )
// C4 = P4-P1  ( parallel to y passing through ox )

For i In {1:8}
  Physical Point(i) = i;
EndFor

For i In {1:8}
  Physical Line(i) = i;
EndFor

Line Loop(9) = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
Line Loop(10) = { 8, 7};

// Create surface mesh (with holes)
 
Plane Surface(1) = {9};
 
Physical Surface(1) = {1};

Plane Surface(2) = {10};
 
Physical Surface(2) = {2};

// vim: set filetype=gmsh :  //
